A small compiler is easiest to extend when it is clear which facts each part owns. A declaration describes a name, a type, and perhaps its storage. A statement consumes those descriptions and chooses operations. A procedure call must faithfully connect the source language's scope rules to the stack layout. Pascal and several members of the Oberon family show how much can be built with a direct mapping between these responsibilities, without assuming that all their dialects make the same choices.

\section{What a Single Pass Needs to Know}

Niklaus Wirth's \emph{The Design of a PASCAL Compiler} describes a self-hosted implementation, including the bootstrap and the layout of code and data. Per Brinch Hansen's \emph{Brinch Hansen on Pascal Compilers} presents a recursive-descent compiler for Pascal*, a teaching dialect, and explicitly distinguishes lexical, syntactic, scope, type, and code-generation tasks. They need not be independent walks over a complete syntax tree. A procedure for parsing a variable declaration can enter a typed symbol and reserve a frame displacement before control returns to the enclosing parser. Wirth's \emph{Compiler Construction} develops the same general method in an exemplary compiler and discusses error recovery alongside it.

Use a one-pass design where the information needed at a use is available at that point. A declared prototype can give a forward call its parameter types even when the target address is still unknown. The unresolved address goes on a fixup list; the call's argument checking does not. In contrast, mutually recursive inferred types require more than an address fixup. They may require finding a group of declarations and solving constraints jointly. A small compiler should say which problem it postpones. When recovering from a broken declaration it can install an error symbol to suppress cascades, but must not leave a plausible frame location for an invalid declaration.

\section{Worked Example: Two Links, Two Jobs}

Wirth's Pascal compiler exposition distinguishes a dynamic link to the caller from a static link that follows source nesting. Consider an inner procedure \texttt{Bump} nested in \texttt{Outer}. It increments \texttt{Outer}'s local \texttt{total}, initialized to zero. In a test, \texttt{Outer} calls \texttt{Bump(2)}; \texttt{Bump(n)} increments once and calls itself when \(n>1\).

\begin{verbatim}
Outer frame: total at displacement d
Bump(2):     caller = Outer,   lexical parent = Outer
Bump(1):     caller = Bump(2), lexical parent = Outer
\end{verbatim}

Both increments use the frame reached by one static-link lookup, then add \(d\). The second call does not pass its own frame as the lexical parent: its caller already has the needed parent pointer. Walking one dynamic link instead would appear to work at the first call and would use the wrong frame at the recursive call. The expected final total is two. Extend the test by recursively entering \texttt{Outer} itself and checking the two independent totals. This detects a compiler that caches the declaring frame globally rather than selecting the appropriate activation.

The code-generation invariant is that following the recorded number of static links reaches the activation of the declaration's lexical scope. The symbol table can record the declaring level and frame displacement, but cannot supply a concrete address for a local of a recursive procedure. A display---a table of current frame pointers indexed by lexical level---can replace repeated link chasing if entry and exit save and restore the appropriate entries.

Figure~\ref{fig:visual-182} shows why the two links cease to be interchangeable on the second activation.

