\section{Small Front End, Explicit Module Contracts}

Changing the language can remove some implementation work rather than merely relocating it. Andreas Pirklbauer's \emph{The Programming Language Oberon-2 (2020 Edition)} explicitly describes a revision based on Oberon-07 and lists the restriction on accessing intermediate objects from nested scopes. This is a property of that specified revision, not a statement about all Oberon dialects. Its type-bound procedure dispatch still needs a way to find the procedure for a receiver's dynamic type, and overriding signatures must match. A small grammar does not make all of the runtime representations trivial.

For a modular implementation, separate the format exported by a compiled module from the symbol table used while compiling its body. An importer needs enough information to check clients and generate correct accesses, but need not receive private local variables. Test this distinction by compiling a client against the exported interface, changing only a provider's private procedure body, and then replacing the compiled provider on the same target. If a public type changes representation, the same test should instead force regeneration or reject the mismatch. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/08-demand-driven-compilation.md}{dossier's Demand-Driven Compilation chapter} develops a different compiler architecture, but likewise depends on knowing which facts a phase can request and which changes invalidate them.

